\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 8. Slide 8\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 To make things more complicated, cancers can also form in the area where the esophagus and stomach meet, called the gastroesophageal junction. (8)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 9. Slide 9\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 The cancers of the gastroesophageal junction behave similar to cancers of the esophagus, so they are often grouped together.\line \line We have other videos that focus on cancer of the esophagus and gastroesophageal junction\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 10. Slide 10\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 This video focuses on cancers of the stomach, or gastric cancers. (10)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 11. Tumor\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Let\u8216?s review some cancer terms. A tumor is an abnormal growth. (11)\line \line Tumors can be either benign or malignant\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 12. Tumor\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 A benign tumor of the stomach may grow over time, but it won\u8217?t ever spread anywhere else (12)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 13. Tumor\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 A malignant tumor of the stomach has the potential to spread elsewhere in the body. This could be to the lymph nodes, the lungs, the liver, so the bone or spread inside the abdominal cavity..(13)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 14. Cancer\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 15. Upper Endoscopy = EGD\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 In most cases, the diagnosis of esophageal cancer is made by endoscopy, also called and EGD for esophago-gastroduodenoscopy (15)\line \line Under sedation, a flexible scope is passed through the mouth into the stomach, which allows viewing the inside of the stomach.\line \line If a tumor is found in the stomach, a small portion can be removed for analysis, which is called a biopsy\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 16. Biopsy\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 The biopsy is examined by a pathologist, who will determine whether the tumor is benign or whether it is malignant, meaning a cancer (16)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 17. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 If the biopsy shows cancer, the next step is staging. Staging is the process of finding out the size of the tumor and whether or not there has been spread to the lymph nodes or other places in the body.(17)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 18. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 The recommended cancer treatment depends upon the stage (18)\line \line Once the stage has been determined, it will be possible to determine the best therapy\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 19. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Staging is going to focus on three areas: (19)\line \line The first is the tumor. How large is the tumor? And more importantly, how deeply has it grown into the wall of the esophagus?\line \line The second is whether there is spread to the lymph nodes.\line \line The third is whether there is spread to other parts of the body\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 20. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 T for Tumor N for Lymph Nodes\line \line And M for metastasis\line \line ...spread to other parts of the body is called metastasis (20)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 21. Slide 21\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 We will start by looking at the wall of the stomach. The wall has multiple layers, as shown here\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 22. Slide 22\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 23. Slide 23\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 24. Slide 24\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 25. Slide 25\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 26. Slide 26\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 27. Slide 27\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 28. Slide 28\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 29. Slide 29\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 30. Slide 30\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 31. Slide 31\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 32. Feeding Tube\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 The inner layer is the mucosa, (32)\line \line followed by the submucosa,\line \line followed by the muscularis, or muscle layer\line \line Outside of the muscularis is the serosa.\line \line Surrounding the stomach are lymph nodes. The purpose of lymph nodes is to filter the blood and help fight infections, but in some cases cancers from the stomach can spread to the lymph nodes\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 33. Slide 33\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 In its earliest stages, cancer of the stomach start in the inner, or most superficial layer, called with mucosa. (33)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 34. Slide 34\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 With time, however, the cancer can continue to grow and invade deeper into the wall of the esophagus. (34)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 35. Slide 35\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 It can continue to silently grow deeper into the wall of the stomach\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 36. Slide 36\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 And can even grow all the way through to the outer layer of the stomach. (36)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 37. Slide 37\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 The deeper the cancer invades into the wall of the esophagus, the more likely it is that cancer cells can spread to lymph nodes (37)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 38. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 To review, the stage consists of 3 parts: (38)\line \line T for Tumor\line \line N for Nodes\line \line M for Metastasis or spread to other organs\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 39. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 We will first consider the tumor (39)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 40. Slide 40\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 T1a tumors invade just the top layer, or mucosa (40)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 41. Slide 41\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 T1b tumors invade into the submucosa (41)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 42. Slide 42\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 T2 tumors invade into the muscularis (42)\line \line \par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 43. Slide 43\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 T3 tumors go all the way throough the muscularis (43)\line \line It can be difficult to tell how deep a tumor has invaded just by looking from the surface.\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 44. Endoscopic Ultrasound\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 For small tumors, endoscopic ultrasound can be helpful. This is an endoscopic procedure done under sedation much like a EGD and it can help determine the T stage of a stomach tumor. (44)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 45. Slide 45\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 The EUS scope has an ultrasound probe on the end, which can be used to examine the tumor (45)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 46. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Evaluation of the lymph nodes is the second part of staging (46)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 47. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Cancers with no signs of spread to the lymph nodes are considered "N-zero" (47)\line \line ...while cancers with signs of spread to the lymph nodes are considered "N-positive"\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 48. CT Scan\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 A CT scan is usually the first test to look for enlarged lymph nodes. (48)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 49. Slide 49\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 50. PET Scan\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 A PET scan is similar to a CT scan. It differs because of a tracer which is injected intravenously which lights up areas of cancer (50)\line \line \par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 51. Slide 51\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 In this example of a PET scan, you can see how the area of the cancer lights up in yellow (51 - PET image)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 52. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 The third part of staging is the M stage, for metastasis. (52)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 53. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 54. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Cancers that have spread to other organs are considered M1 (54)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 55. Liver\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 There are several common sites to which gastric cancer can spread, such as the liver (55)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 56. Lungs\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 ... lungs (56)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 57. Bone\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 ... or bones (57). The CT scan and PET scans are used to make sure there are no signs of spread.\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 58. Peritoneum\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 An additional pattern of potential spread is carcinomatosis (58). This is spread inside the abdominal cavity.\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 59. Peritoneum\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 The peritoneum is the lining of the abdominal cavity.\line \line Gastric cancer cells can break off from the stomach and attach to the peritoneum and grow.\line \line This is known as peritoneal metastasis or carcinomatosis.\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 60. Peritoneal Metastasis\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Peritoneal metastasis can be very difficult to detect, because the disease often consists of multiple small nodules \line No larger than a grain of rice, and frequently can't be seen on CT or PET scans. (59)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 61. Peritoneal Metastasis\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Detection of peritoneal metastasis requires looking inside the abdodminal cavity with a minor surgical procedure called laparoscopy (60)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 62. Laparoscopy\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Laparoscopy is a minor surgical procedure performed under a general anesthetic and it\u8217?s usually done as an outpatient\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 63. Laparoscopy\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Several small incisions are made, and a instrument the diameter of a pencil is inserted to examine the inside of the abdomen\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 64. Slide 64\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 A laparoscope is an instrument used to look inside the abdominal cavity (63)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 65. Slide 65\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Laparoscopy allows the detection of peritoneal metastasis within the abdomen (64)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 66. Staging\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 To review, staging consists of three parts: T for Tumor N for Nodes and M for Metastasis (65)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 67. Treatment\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 We will briefly summarize treatments:\line \line Superficial cancers are T1 and limited to the mucosa. These can usually be treated with endoscopic therapy alone. Surgery can be avoided in many of these cases. (66)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 68. Treatment\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Localized cancers are T2 tumors that invade the muscle and are treated with surgery, also termed gastrectomy (67)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 69. Treatment\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Locally-advanced cancers are T3 or Node-positive and are usually treated with chemotherapy first, followed by surgery (68)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 70. Treatment\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Metastatic cancers are treated initially with intravenous chemotherapy. (69)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 71. Treatment\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 This allows cancer-killing drugs to circulate through the bloodstream (70)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 72. Metastastic Disease (M1)\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 As we discussed earlier, there are a number of different sites where gastric cancer can spread:\line Liver\line Lungs\line Bone\line Or inside the abdominal cavity. This is referred to as the peritoneum\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 73. Peritoneal Metastasis (M1)\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 After initial treatment with chemotherapy, some patients are candidates for additional therapy (71)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 74. Peritoneal Metastasis (M1)\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Surgery is used in some patients with metastatic disease after initial chemotherapy. (72)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 75. Peritoneal Metastasis (M1)\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Gastrectomy can be used to remove the tumor in the stomach (73)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 76. Peritoneal Metastasis (M1)\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Cytoreductive surgery can be used to remove areas of spread of cancer within the abdomen (74)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 77. Peritoneal Metastasis (M1)\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 In some patients, chemotherapy can be administered into the abdominal cavity, also known as intraperitoneal chemotherapy (75)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 78. Peritoneal Metastasis (M1)\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Intraperitoneal chemotherapy can be administered as a surgical procedure called HIPEC (76)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 79. Peritoneal Metastasis (M1)\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 Intraperitoneal chemotherapy can also be administered as an outpatient after surgical placement of a intraperitoneal catheter (77)\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s2\b\f0\fs28\outlinelevel1 80. Slide 80\b0\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22 We are working on preparing videos to address each of these different treatments, so please subscribe to be notified when we post new videos.\line \line (pause)\line \line We hope you have found this video helpful. This videos and others like it are designed to\line \line educate patients and families about gastric cancer\line \line Feel free to leave a comment about your experience with gastric cancer or if you have suggestions for new videos.\par\par\pard\s0\f0\fs22\par}